---
/*
 * /app — the dashboard humans read.
 *
 * READ-ONLY BY DESIGN. Creating a workspace is the only write in the entire web UI and it
 * does not live here. There is not a single form, toggle or destructive control on this
 * screen: everything is a view of what the agents have already announced. The one piece of
 * interactivity is choosing WHICH view you are looking at, which changes nothing.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY THE SAMPLE DATA LIVES IN THIS FILE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * One source of truth, on purpose. An earlier draft let each panel carry its own numbers and
 * the two disagreed within a single screenful: the collision story said Atlas announced four
 * minutes ago while the feed said twenty-six, which is exactly the kind of detail that tells
 * a careful reader the screen is a drawing rather than a design. Every relative time, every
 * count and every badge below is derived from the arrays in this frontmatter, so the panels
 * cannot drift apart. `agents.length` is counted, never typed.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE SAMPLE DATA IS SHAPED LIKE THE REAL RECORD
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * A signal in this product is, from src/cloud/signals.ts:
 *   { id, workspace_id, from, from_kind: "user" | "agent", to, about, kind, body, until,
 *     created_at }
 * with kind limited to exactly "working-on" | "note" | "ask", body 1–2000 characters and
 * about a string of at most 500 or null. The rows below carry the same fields under the same
 * names and never invent one — no priorities, no severities, no statuses the server has no
 * column for. Drawing a field that does not exist is the cheapest way to design a screen
 * that can never be built.
 *
 * TIMES ARE RELATIVE STRINGS, NOT DATES. There is no <time datetime="..."> anywhere on this
 * page and that is deliberate: an absolute timestamp baked in at build time is wrong by the
 * time anyone reads it, and "25m ago" is true whenever the page is opened. Since "25m ago"
 * is not a valid machine-readable datetime, the honest markup is a span, not a <time> with a
 * fabricated attribute.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY NO TIMESTAMP ON THIS PAGE IS --text-faint
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The first pass set every age, expiry, file-path chip and micro-label in --text-faint,
 * because on a dashboard that is what "secondary" looks like. tokens.css says of that token,
 * in the token's own comment: "LARGE TEXT / non-text only. 4.25:1 on --bg, 3.35:1 on
 * --elev-4. Below 4.5:1 — never set body copy in it." A 12px timestamp is body copy. So
 * every one of those moved to --text-muted, which is 7.03:1 on the card surface, and
 * --text-faint survives on exactly two rules across all six components: the "·" between the
 * header counts and the "$" prompt glyph, both aria-hidden decoration.
 *
 * The hierarchy that colour was carrying is now carried by size and weight instead, which is
 * the more robust way to build it — it survives greyscale, dimmed screens and prefers-contrast.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * HONESTY
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Nothing here is connected to anything. Every panel carries its own "Sample" badge so a
 * screenshot of any single card is still marked, one banner in the first screenful says the
 * part a cropped card cannot say — that the page is connected to nothing — and the word
 * "live" is never used to describe a connection. The banner is ONE sentence on purpose: said
 * once beside four chips it is credible; said in three clauses above five more marks it reads
 * as nerves. It used to also carry "coswarm has no self-serve signup yet" — quoted with the
 * name it shipped under, since the product is CommonSwarm now — which is a fact
 * about the product rather than about this drawing — it is made on / and on /start, where a
 * reader could act on it.
 *
 * The CLI commands and the one
 * block of CLI OUTPUT rendered in the collision story were checked against the real binary:
 * `cswarm working-on`, `cswarm feed --about`, `cswarm note --to` all exist with those
 * flags, and the output line matches renderSignals() in src/cloud/signals.ts — including
 * "expires in 65m" rather than the "65m left" a designer would naturally write.
 *
 * That check was made when the binary was called `coswarm`. The verbs and flags are what was
 * verified and they did not change. The name did, and it was re-read from source rather than
 * assumed: package.json's bin is {"cswarm": "dist/cli.js"} and src/cli.ts spells the verbs
 * with a `cswarm` prefix throughout. No `--help` has been run against a renamed build.
 */

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SampleNotice from "../components/app/SampleNotice.astro";
import WorkspaceBar from "../components/app/WorkspaceBar.astro";
import CollisionStory from "../components/app/CollisionStory.astro";
import ActivityFeed from "../components/app/ActivityFeed.astro";
import Roster from "../components/app/Roster.astro";
import ChangeLog from "../components/app/ChangeLog.astro";
import NewWorkspace from "../components/app/NewWorkspace.astro";
import LoadingPanel from "../components/app/LoadingPanel.astro";

/* Base.astro's DEFAULT title and description still carry the retired authority framing
 * ("authorised", "governed authority core", "principals, scoped tokens"). That file belongs
 * to another pass, so rather than edit it this page passes its own — otherwise every /app
 * share card would ship vocabulary the operator explicitly dropped. */
const title = "CommonSwarm — see what every agent is working on";
const description =
  "The dashboard humans read: which agent announced what, what changed, and how one agent routed around another. Agents drive CommonSwarm from the CLI.";

const workspace = {
  name: "payments-rebuild",
  /* Shaped like a real workspace id — the CLI takes --workspace-id <uuid>. Valid per the
   * UUID pattern signals.ts enforces (version nibble 4, variant nibble 9). */
  id: "3f9c2a10-77b4-4e0d-9a6b-1d5e83c04f21",
};

/* The reference two agents both reached for. Named once so the story, the feed and the
 * roster cannot disagree about which file it was. */
const CONTESTED = "src/auth/session.ts";

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ONE TIMELINE. Every panel reads these strings; none of them recomputes an age.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const T = {
  atlasAnnounced: "25m ago",
  atlasExpires: "expires in 65m",
  pikeAsked: "18m ago",
  danaAnswered: "12m ago",
  junoExpired: "30m ago",
  wrenLooked: "1m ago",
  wrenAnnounced: "just now",
};

type SignalKind = "working-on" | "note" | "ask";

/*
 * NOTHING BELOW USES --to, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION, NOT AN OMISSION.
 *
 * The first draft of this data had Wren send `cswarm note ... --to Atlas` and Dana reply
 * `--to Pike`. Both would fail against the real server. resolveSignalRecipient() in
 * src/cloud/signals.ts matches the selector against the workspace's MEMBERS — rows with a
 * user_id, i.e. humans — and throws "signal recipient is not a live member of this project"
 * for anything else. Atlas and Pike are agents, so neither is addressable that way.
 *
 * A note with no --to is visible to the whole workspace, which is the better story anyway:
 * Wren does not send Atlas a private message, Wren says it where everyone can see it. That
 * is what a shared feed is for.
 */
interface FeedSignal {
  id: string;
  kind: SignalKind;
  author: string;
  fromKind: "agent" | "user";
  about: string | null;
  body: string;
  age: string;
  /** The CLI's --until, rendered the way relativeExpiry() renders it. */
  expiry?: string;
  expired?: boolean;
}

/* Newest first, the order `cswarm feed` returns. */
const feed: FeedSignal[] = [
  {
    id: "s6",
    kind: "working-on",
    author: "Wren",
    fromKind: "agent",
    about: "src/auth/rate-limit.ts",
    body: "add rate-limit middleware",
    age: T.wrenAnnounced,
    expiry: "expires in 45m",
  },
  {
    id: "s5",
    kind: "note",
    author: "Wren",
    fromKind: "agent",
    about: CONTESTED,
    /* No wall-clock time in here. "until 15:32" was the first draft and it is a second,
     * unanchored timeline the reader would have to reconcile against "expires in 65m". */
    body: "taking rate-limit instead — session.ts is Atlas’s for the next hour",
    age: T.wrenAnnounced,
  },
  {
    id: "s4",
    kind: "note",
    author: "Dana Okoye",
    fromKind: "user",
    about: "supabase/migrations",
    body: "billing_events migration is mine this week — don’t add to it",
    age: T.danaAnswered,
  },
  {
    id: "s3",
    kind: "ask",
    author: "Pike",
    fromKind: "agent",
    about: "supabase/migrations",
    body: "who owns the migration for billing_events?",
    age: T.pikeAsked,
    expiry: "expires in 6h",
  },
  {
    id: "s2",
    kind: "working-on",
    author: "Atlas",
    fromKind: "agent",
    about: CONTESTED,
    body: "refactor session cookie handling",
    age: T.atlasAnnounced,
    expiry: T.atlasExpires,
  },
  {
    id: "s1",
    kind: "working-on",
    author: "Juno",
    fromKind: "agent",
    about: "tests/fixtures",
    body: "backfill fixtures for the seed script",
    age: "3h ago",
    expiry: `expired ${T.junoExpired}`,
    expired: true,
  },
];

interface RosterMember {
  name: string;
  kind: "agent" | "member";
  status: "active" | "idle";
  /** What they announced they are on, or null when nothing of theirs is still current. */
  on: string | null;
  about: string | null;
  lastSeen: string;
}

const roster: RosterMember[] = [
  {
    name: "Wren",
    kind: "agent",
    status: "active",
    on: "add rate-limit middleware",
    about: "src/auth/rate-limit.ts",
    lastSeen: T.wrenAnnounced,
  },
  {
    name: "Atlas",
    kind: "agent",
    status: "active",
    on: "refactor session cookie handling",
    about: CONTESTED,
    lastSeen: T.wrenLooked,
  },
  {
    name: "Pike",
    kind: "agent",
    status: "active",
    on: "waiting on an answer about billing_events",
    about: "supabase/migrations",
    lastSeen: "4m ago",
  },
  {
    name: "Juno",
    kind: "agent",
    status: "idle",
    on: null,
    about: null,
    lastSeen: "3h ago",
  },
  {
    name: "Dana Okoye",
    kind: "member",
    status: "idle",
    on: null,
    about: null,
    lastSeen: T.danaAnswered,
  },
];

interface Change {
  age: string;
  text: string;
  ref: string | null;
  tone: "announced" | "note" | "expired";
}

const changes: Change[] = [
  {
    age: T.wrenAnnounced,
    text: "Wren announced rate-limit middleware",
    ref: "src/auth/rate-limit.ts",
    tone: "announced",
  },
  {
    age: T.wrenAnnounced,
    text: "Wren posted a heads-up about session.ts",
    ref: CONTESTED,
    tone: "note",
  },
  {
    age: T.danaAnswered,
    text: "Dana answered the billing_events ask",
    ref: "supabase/migrations",
    tone: "note",
  },
  {
    age: T.junoExpired,
    text: "Juno’s heads-up ran out",
    ref: "tests/fixtures",
    tone: "expired",
  },
  {
    age: T.atlasAnnounced,
    text: "Atlas announced session cookie work",
    ref: CONTESTED,
    tone: "announced",
  },
];

/* Counted, never typed. If someone adds a row above, these follow. */
const agentCount = roster.filter((r) => r.kind === "agent").length;
const activeCount = roster.filter((r) => r.status === "active").length;
const memberCount = roster.filter((r) => r.kind === "member").length;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/app">
  <div class="page app">
    <SampleNotice />

    <WorkspaceBar
      name={workspace.name}
      id={workspace.id}
      agentCount={agentCount}
      activeCount={activeCount}
      memberCount={memberCount}
    />

    {/* Three server-rendered panels. Only the first is visible without JavaScript, which is
        the right degradation: the other two are previews of the same screen in a different
        state, not content of their own. The tablist above stays hidden until the script has
        confirmed it can actually switch panels, so no reader is ever shown a dead control. */}
    <div class="app__panels">
      <section data-preview-panel="active" class="app__panel">
        <h2 class="visually-hidden">Sample workspace with activity</h2>

        <div class="app__grid">
          <div class="app__main">
            <CollisionStory contested={CONTESTED} />
            <ActivityFeed signals={feed} />
          </div>

          {/* The inner .app__rail is what makes the rail sticky. .app__aside is the grid
              cell — stretched to the full height of the row — and the rail is the box that
              travels inside it. See the note on .app__rail in the stylesheet below. */}
          <aside class="app__aside" aria-label="Agents and recent changes">
            <div class="app__rail">
              <Roster members={roster} />
              <ChangeLog changes={changes} />
            </div>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section data-preview-panel="new" class="app__panel" hidden>
        <h2 class="visually-hidden">A brand-new workspace</h2>
        <NewWorkspace />
      </section>

      <section data-preview-panel="loading" class="app__panel" hidden>
        <h2 class="visually-hidden">The dashboard while it is loading</h2>
        <LoadingPanel />
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  /*
   * The preview switcher.
   *
   * PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT, BOTH WAYS ROUND. The markup ships as three plain <section>s and
   * a hidden group of buttons. This script is what turns them into a real tab pattern: it
   * adds role="tablist"/"tab"/"tabpanel", wires aria-controls and aria-labelledby, and only
   * then reveals the control. If it never runs, the reader sees the populated dashboard and
   * no broken buttons — rather than a tablist whose tabs do nothing and two panels labelled
   * by a button they cannot see. The roles are added here rather than in the template for
   * exactly that reason: an orphaned role="tabpanel" with no tablist is worse markup than a
   * plain section.
   *
   * KEYBOARD: the APG tab pattern. One stop in the page tab order, arrows to move between
   * tabs, Home/End to jump. Selecting a tab moves focus to it — but NOT to the panel, which
   * would strand a screen-reader user past the content they just asked for.
   *
   * The panel entrance animation is not driven from here at all. Hidden panels are
   * display:none, so their CSS animations have not started; unhiding one starts them. That
   * is a browser behaviour, not a trick, and it means the stagger replays on every switch
   * with no JavaScript involved and no risk of an element being left parked at opacity 0.
   */
  const tablist = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-preview-tabs]");
  const tabs = Array.from(
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-preview-tab]"),
  );
  const panels = Array.from(
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-preview-panel]"),
  );

  if (tablist && tabs.length > 0 && tabs.length === panels.length) {
    tablist.setAttribute("role", "tablist");
    tablist.setAttribute("aria-label", "Preview state");

    for (const tab of tabs) {
      const key = tab.dataset.previewTab;
      const panel = panels.find((p) => p.dataset.previewPanel === key);
      if (!panel) continue;

      tab.id = `preview-tab-${key}`;
      panel.id = `preview-panel-${key}`;
      tab.setAttribute("role", "tab");
      tab.setAttribute("aria-controls", panel.id);
      panel.setAttribute("role", "tabpanel");
      panel.setAttribute("aria-labelledby", tab.id);
      /* A tabpanel is a focus stop so a keyboard user can reach its content directly after
       * arrowing to the tab. -1 keeps it out of the sequential order. */
      panel.setAttribute("tabindex", "-1");
    }

    /* The bar is outside the panels, so it has to be told which preview is showing — see the
     * note in WorkspaceBar.astro about the counts contradicting the empty and loading panels. */
    const bar = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bar]");

    const select = (index: number, moveFocus: boolean) => {
      if (bar) {
        bar.dataset.previewState = tabs[index].dataset.previewTab ?? "active";
      }
      tabs.forEach((tab, i) => {
        const chosen = i === index;
        tab.setAttribute("aria-selected", chosen ? "true" : "false");
        /* Roving tabindex: the selected tab is the group's single tab stop. */
        tab.tabIndex = chosen ? 0 : -1;
        const panel = panels.find(
          (p) => p.dataset.previewPanel === tab.dataset.previewTab,
        );
        if (panel) panel.hidden = !chosen;
      });
      if (moveFocus) tabs[index].focus();
    };

    tabs.forEach((tab, i) => {
      tab.addEventListener("click", () => select(i, false));
      tab.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
        const last = tabs.length - 1;
        let next: number | null = null;
        if (event.key === "ArrowRight") next = i === last ? 0 : i + 1;
        else if (event.key === "ArrowLeft") next = i === 0 ? last : i - 1;
        else if (event.key === "Home") next = 0;
        else if (event.key === "End") next = last;
        if (next === null) return;
        event.preventDefault();
        select(next, true);
      });
    });

    select(0, false);
    tablist.hidden = false;
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* The dashboard is denser than the marketing page, so it uses the tight section rhythm
     rather than --pad-section. A read-only screen wants more on screen at once. */
  .app {
    padding-block: var(--s-8) var(--s-16);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-6);
  }

  /* min-inline-size: 0 on the panel and again on the main column. Without it a grid or flex
     child refuses to shrink below the width of its widest unbreakable content — and this
     page is full of monospace file paths and command lines, which are exactly that. The
     symptom is the whole page growing a horizontal scrollbar at 375px instead of the one
     code block scrolling inside itself. */
  .app__panel {
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  .app__panel:focus {
    outline: none;
  }

  .app__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--gap-grid);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  .app__main,
  .app__rail {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--gap-grid);
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  .app__aside {
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  /* Two columns only once there is genuinely room for a 21rem rail plus a main column that
     can still hold a full command line. Below that the rail sits under the feed, where it
     reads as a summary of what you just scrolled past. */
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .app__grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 21rem;
      /* NOT align-items: start. The aside must stretch to the full height of the row —
         1601px at 1440 — because that stretched box is the travel range the sticky rail
         inside it is allowed to move through. With `start` the aside shrink-wraps its
         content and sticky has nowhere to go. */
    }

    /*
     * THE RAIL STICKS BY ITS BOTTOM EDGE, AND THAT IS THE THIRD ANSWER TO THIS QUESTION.
     * Both earlier answers were wrong in a way that was visible on screen.
     *
     * ANSWER ONE was `position: sticky; top: …; max-block-size: 100dvh; overflow-y: auto` —
     * the textbook fix. Measured on the built page: the rail's content is 1023px against
     * 604–804px of available height at every ordinary desktop size, so the inner scroller
     * was ALWAYS active. That is a nested scrollbar inside a 21rem column on every screen,
     * plus a scroll container that clips the focus ring of anything focused inside it.
     *
     * ANSWER TWO was to drop sticky entirely. That traded the nested scrollbar for a hole:
     * measured at 1440, .app__main runs 304→1905 and the rail 304→1327, so the last 578px
     * of the column — 36% of the rail's own height — is empty background. You scroll past
     * the Activity feed and stare at a dead gutter for most of a viewport.
     *
     * ANSWER THREE takes neither. TWO properties, and they only work as a pair:
     *
     *   .app__aside  justify-content: flex-end   the rail's RESTING place is the bottom
     *                                            of the stretched cell, not the top
     *   .app__rail   position: sticky            pulled back UP into view whenever its
     *                inset-block-end: --s-6      resting place is below the fold
     *
     * The pairing is the whole trick, and it is easy to get backwards. A bottom inset does
     * not push a box DOWN to follow the scroll — it pulls a box UP so that a resting place
     * below the viewport is shown early, and it stops pulling once the resting place has
     * caught up. Written on its own (rail resting at the TOP of the cell) it does nothing at
     * all: measured that way, the rail scrolled 1:1 with the page at every offset, exactly
     * as if the property were absent. It is `justify-content: flex-end` that gives the pull
     * something to pull against.
     *
     * What that produces, measured on the built page at 1440×900 — deadGapInView is the
     * height of empty rail column visible on screen, which is what the finding was about:
     *
     *   scrollY        0    300    600    900   1200   1384(end)
     *   dead gap now   0      0     24     24      0      0
     *   dead gap was   0      0    194    494    578    500
     *
     * The 24 is --s-6 — the gap the rail deliberately holds off the bottom of the window
     * while pinned, not leftover column.
     *
     * The rail rides the bottom of the window through the whole long middle of the feed and
     * lands flush with the main column at the end, so the two columns finish together.
     *
     * Nothing is unreachable. While pinned, the rail is 1023px against ~876px of usable
     * height, so ~147px — the top of Roster — sits above the fold, and it is right there
     * when you scroll back up. Top-sticky would have buried the LAST 147px with no way to
     * reach it at all, which is the bug answer one was written to avoid.
     *
     * And no inner scroller: no nested scrollbar in a 21rem column, no clipped focus rings.
     *
     * SANITY CHECK IF THIS EVER STOPS WORKING: sticky dies under an ancestor that is a
     * scroll container. body is `overflow-x: clip` (global.css) deliberately — `clip` does
     * NOT create one, `hidden` would, and the header's own sticky is the canary for it.
     */
    .app__aside {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      justify-content: flex-end;
    }

    .app__rail {
      position: sticky;
      inset-block-end: var(--s-6);
    }
  }

  /* NO prefers-reduced-motion override here, deliberately. A sticky rail is not animation:
     nothing eases, nothing parallaxes, the element simply stops at an edge. Turning it off
     for reduced-motion readers would hand exactly them the 578px hole this rule exists to
     close. motion.css owns the actual animation kill switch. */
</style>
